      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "4\n3 0\n1 3 3\n3 1\n1 3 3\n4 2\n25 40 47 30\n5 1\n120 100 82 111 74",
        "output": "1\n0\n4\n19",
        "explanation": "Explanation:\nTest case 111: K=0K = 0K=0, so there cannot be any temperature changes.\nIt's optimal for Chef to choose B=[2,2,2]B = [2, 2, 2]B=[2,2,2].\nThis gives:\n\n∣A1−B1∣=∣1−2∣=1|A_1 - B_1| = |1 - 2| = 1∣A1​−B1​∣=∣1−2∣=1\n∣A2−B2∣=∣3−2∣=1|A_2 - B_2| = |3 - 2| = 1∣A2​−B2​∣=∣3−2∣=1\n∣A3−B3∣=∣3−2∣=1|A_3 - B_3| = |3 - 2| = 1∣A3​−B3​∣=∣3−2∣=1\n\nAll differences are 111, so the unsavoriness - the maximum difference - is 111. This is optimal.\nTest case 222: Same as the previous case, but K=1K = 1K=1 so one temperature change is allowed.\nChef can choose B=[1,3,3]B = [1, 3, 3]B=[1,3,3], the same as AAA, which has only one temperature change. The unsavoriness is 000 since Ai=BiA_i = B_iAi​=Bi​ for all iii.\nTest case 333: One optimal choice of BBB is B=[27,44,44,27]B = [27, 44, 44, 27]B=[27,44,44,27]. There are two temperature changes (at steps 222 and 444), which is allowed because K=2K = 2K=2.\nIt can be verified that ∣A2−B2∣=4|A_2 - B_2| = 4∣A2​−B2​∣=4 is the maximum difference. 444 is optimal.\nTest case 444: One optimal choice of BBB is B=[101,101,101,101,90]B = [101, 101, 101, 101, 90]B=[101,101,101,101,90], which has one temperature change."
      }
